You can use the following PHP code to rearrange an array of integers as described:
```
function rearrangeArray($array) {
  $n = count($array);
  $j = 0;

  for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
    if ($array[$i] < 0) {
      if ($i != $j) {
        $temp = $array[$i];
        $array[$i] = $array[$j];
        $array[$j] = $temp;
      }
      $j++;
    }
  }

  return $array;
}
$array = array(3, -4, 2, -1, 7);
$result = rearrangeArray($array);
print_r($result);
```
This code uses a two-pointer approach to iterate through the array. The variable $j keeps track of the index of the first non-negative integer in the array. The loop checks if the current integer is negative, and if so, swaps it with the integer at index $j and increments $j. This effectively moves all negative integers to the front of the array, while preserving their original order. Finally, the function returns the modified array.

