Here is some sample code that demonstrates how to generate a random terrain with a river system using Java:
```
public class TerrainGenerator {
    private static final int TERRAIN_SIZE = 512;
    private static final double RIVER_WIDTH = 20.0;
    private static final double RIVER_DEPTH = 10.0;
    private static final double RIVER_CURVE = 0.02;
    private static final double RIVER_START_VARIATION = 0.1;
    private static final double RIVER_PATH_VARIATION = 0.1;

    private double[][] terrain;
    private Random random;

    public TerrainGenerator() {
        terrain = new double[TERRAIN_SIZE][TERRAIN_SIZE];
        random = new Random();
    }

    public double[][] generateTerrain() {
        // Generate the terrain using Perlin noise
        for (int x = 0; x < TERRAIN_SIZE; x++) {
            for (int y = 0; y < TERRAIN_SIZE; y++) {
                terrain[x][y] = PerlinNoise.noise(x, y);
            }
        }

        // Find the lowest point on the terrain
        double lowestPoint = Double.MAX_VALUE;
        int lowestX = 0;
        int lowestY = 0;
        for (int x = 0; x < TERRAIN_SIZE; x++) {
            for (int y = 0; y < TERRAIN_SIZE; y++) {
                if (terrain[x][y] < lowestPoint) {
                    lowestPoint = terrain[x][y];
                    lowestX = x;
                    lowestY = y;
                }
            }
        }

        // Create the river
        double riverX = lowestX + random.nextDouble() * RIVER_START_VARIATION;
        double riverY = lowestY;
        while (riverX >= 0 && riverX < TERRAIN_SIZE && riverY >= 0 && riverY < TERRAIN_SIZE) {
            // Adjust the height of the terrain around the river
            for (int x = (int) (riverX - RIVER_WIDTH); x <= riverX + RIVER_WIDTH; x++) {
                for (int y = (int) (riverY - RIVER_WIDTH); y <= riverY + RIVER_WIDTH; y++) {
                    if (x >= 0 && x < TERRAIN_SIZE && y >= 0 && y < TERRAIN_SIZE) {
                        double distance = Math.sqrt((x - riverX) * (x - riverX) + (y - riverY) * (y - riverY));
                        double depth = RIVER_DEPTH * Math.exp(-distance / RIVER_CURVE);
                        terrain[x][y] -= depth;
                    }
                }
            }

            // Move the river in a random direction
            double dx = random.nextDouble() * RIVER_PATH_VARIATION * 2 - RIVER_PATH_VARIATION;
            double dy = random.nextDouble() * RIVER_PATH_VARIATION * 2 - RIVER_PATH_VARIATION;
            riverX += dx;
            riverY += dy;
        }

        return terrain;
    }
}
```
This code uses Perlin noise to generate the terrain and then creates a river by adjusting the height of the terrain around it. The river path is randomly generated using a combination of Perlin noise and a random number generator. The width and depth of the river are also varied using a combination of Perlin noise and a random number generator. Finally, the starting point of the river is randomly varied to ensure that no two rivers are identical.

