To validate and verify an email address in PHP, you can use a combination of regular expressions, DNS lookup, and SMTP communication. Here is an example code snippet that checks if an email address is valid, exists on the email server, and is not from a disposable email provider:
```
$email = 'example@email.com';
// Check if email address is valid
if (!filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)) {
    echo "Invalid email address";
    exit;
}
// Check if domain is not a disposable email provider
$disposable_domains = array('example1.com', 'example2.com', 'example3.com'); // Add more domains as needed
$domain = explode('@', $email)[1];
if (in_array($domain, $disposable_domains)) {
    echo "Disposable email address";
    exit;
}
// Check if email address exists on email server
list($user, $domain) = explode('@', $email);
if (!checkdnsrr($domain, 'MX')) {
    echo "Invalid email domain";
    exit;
}
$smtp_socket = fsockopen($domain, 25, $errno, $errstr, 10);
if (!$smtp_socket) {
    echo "Could not connect to email server";
    exit;
}
$response = fgets($smtp_socket);
if (strpos($response, '220') !== 0) {
    echo "Unexpected response from email server";
    exit;
}
fputs($smtp_socket, "HELO $domain\r\n");
$response = fgets($smtp_socket);
if (strpos($response, '250') !== 0) {
    echo "Unexpected response from email server";
    exit;
}
fputs($smtp_socket, "MAIL FROM: <$email>\r\n");
$response = fgets($smtp_socket);
if (strpos($response, '250') !== 0) {
    echo "Unexpected response from email server";
    exit;
}
fputs($smtp_socket, "RCPT TO: <$email>\r\n");
$response = fgets($smtp_socket);
if (strpos($response, '250') !== 0) {
    echo "Email address does not exist on email server";
    exit;
}
fclose($smtp_socket);
echo "Email address is valid and exists on email server";
```
Note that this code snippet only checks the existence of the email address on the email server, not its actual deliverability. Also, the list of disposable email providers is not exhaustive, so you may need to update it as needed.

Sure, here's a Java function that sorts an array of colors in RGB format based on their hue value in the HSL color model:
```java
import java.awt.Color;
import java.util.Arrays;
public class ColorSorter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Color[] colors = { new Color(128, 0, 128), // purple
                           new Color(0, 0, 255),    // blue
                           new Color(255, 255, 0),  // yellow
                           new Color(255, 0, 0) };  // red
        sortColorsByHue(colors);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(colors));
    }
    public static void sortColorsByHue(Color[] colors) {
        Arrays.sort(colors, (c1, c2) -> {
            float[] hsb1 = Color.RGBtoHSB(c1.getRed(), c1.getGreen(), c1.getBlue(), null);
            float[] hsb2 = Color.RGBtoHSB(c2.getRed(), c2.getGreen(), c2.getBlue(), null);
            return Float.compare(hsb1[0], hsb2[0]);
        });
    }
}
```
The `sortColorsByHue` function takes an array of `Color` objects and sorts them in place based on their hue value in the HSL color model. It uses the `Color.RGBtoHSB` method to convert each color to the HSB color model, which represents colors in terms of hue, saturation, and brightness. It then compares the hue values of each color using the `Float.compare` method, which returns a negative, zero, or positive integer if the first argument is less than, equal to, or greater than the second argument, respectively. The `Arrays.sort` method sorts the colors in ascending order based on this comparison. Finally, the sorted array is printed using the `Arrays.toString` method.

